  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a cross-sectional design and self-reported walking data from a web-based survey, which limits causal claims.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the relationship between a postcode-level walkability index (JPWI) and walking habits using survey data from 40,286 Japanese adults across 95 cities in 2021. The JPWI was calculated using population density, intersection density, and destination counts within a 1000 m road network buffer. The analysis revealed non-linear positive associations for total, leisure, and transport walking, identifying a critical inflection point at a JPWI score of 0.78, above which walking times increased sharply.",
      "why": "It establishes specific, empirical thresholds where built environment improvements actually begin to trigger significant increases in physical activity, moving beyond simple linear assumptions of walkability benefits."
    }
  },
